{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/connector at 9f30db4245ea2831297de51840343e9e3699b690 on request/github-connector-render. Successor to bd298154c8f37edc27872f04c9d3a1b2768cd48b.\n\nThe predecessor fixed clause admission and left the identical defect one layer up, which made the fix untestable in practice. charterIsLive in cmd/gitseq-github/main.go refused a stale charter before clause admission was ever reached. Every charter an operator states correctly is stale from the moment it is written — a replacement must cite what it replaces, and what it replaces is then retired — so this workroom's scoped charter would still have been rejected at the door and the dogfood would still have been blocked. I reported the predecessor as unblocking the dogfood; that claim was false and is corrected in assert d1811439cf13f91ce8a0f5449380b696d0c16da9.\n\nStaleness at the charter gate is now reported to whoever runs the connector rather than hiding the charter. Retirement and absent ratification still refuse, because those are withdrawals of authority rather than movement underneath it.\n\nThe proposal coherence check is deliberately left refusing a stale request or artifact. That asymmetry is a judgement, not an oversight: a pull request naming a stale artifact tells a public reader it is current when its basis has moved, and unlike a charter nothing makes an artifact stale by construction. Staleness therefore blocks in one place and not in two, and a reviewer should decide whether that line is in the right spot.\n\nThe expectation that a stale charter is refused is replaced rather than deleted, so the reason the old rule existed stays readable beside the reason it changed. Mutation-checked by restoring the refusal, which fails the test.\n\ngo vet and the full suite pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"branch":"request/github-connector-render","commit":"9f30db4245ea2831297de51840343e9e3699b690","head":"9f30db4245ea2831297de51840343e9e3699b690","path":"internal/connector"}}